<!-- YAM teaching handle v2: the v1 passive two-part pistol grip with the butt of the shell
     truncated. Geometry, frames, masses and inertias come from the ONShape export of the YAM +
     short-teaching-handle assembly ("YAM  handle short"), whose arm chain carries the same
     link1..link5 masses and inertias as arm/yam/v1/yam.urdf, so its `dof_joint6` transform is
     exactly this file's flange to handle transform.

     What differs from v1, and nothing else does:

       * the shell is 38.2 mm shorter. Its bounding box is identical in x and y (79.13 x 99.11 mm)
         and identical at the flange end; the whole difference is at the butt, where min z moves
         from -214.452 mm to -176.247 mm in the handle frame. Mass drops 14.0 g to 0.238592 kg and
         the shell COM moves 17.2 mm back toward the flange.
       * the trigger is the *same part*, re-clocked +16 deg about the hinge axis. Its mass agrees
         to five digits, its principal moments to 1e-5 relative, and rotating the v1 trigger's
         vertex cloud by Rx(+16 deg) about the pivot reproduces this one's bounding box to
         0.006 mm. Only its rest orientation changed.
       * the trigger pivot sits 0.51 mm further out: 0.1019915745 m from the flange rather than
         0.1014822104 m.
       * total mass is 0.243591 kg rather than 0.257594 kg.
       * `tcp_site` follows the shorter butt: defined as for v1 (the shell's furthest point along
         the approach axis), it sits 176.0 mm from the flange rather than 208.4 mm.

     Everything else is identical to v1 and derived in ../v1/yam_teaching_handle.xml's header:
     the root body named `gripper` and rotated Rx(+135 deg) from the flange, the `tcp_site`
     definition and orientation, and the passive trigger hinge named `gripper` with the same axis
     and range.
     Masses and inertias are this export's verbatim values; the assembly carries real materials.

     The trigger's `quat` below is its principal-axis frame with the export's `ixy` and `ixz`
     zeroed first: at 3e-14 and 1e-13 against a largest moment of 7e-07 those are CAD symmetry
     noise, and zeroing them makes x exactly a principal axis. That yields `diaginertia`'s middle
     entry exactly equal to the export's `ixx`, and a quat with x == y and w == -z. That symmetry
     is preserved exactly by any rotation about x, which is why v1's trigger quat has the same
     structure. The shell's off-diagonals are real coupling and are kept.

     Geom colours are this export's own materials, so the v2 shell renders orange where v1's is
     blue. -->
<mujoco model="yam_teaching_handle">
  <compiler angle="radian" meshdir="assets"/>

  <asset>
    <mesh name="handle" file="handle.stl"/>
    <mesh name="trigger" file="trigger.stl"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <body name="gripper" pos="0 0 0" quat="0.38268 0.92388 0 0">
      <inertial pos="0.00222058 0.0206182 -0.0630398" quat="0.700697371576107 -0.0328033925254197 -0.705130845057546 -0.103622498780861" mass="0.238592" diaginertia="0.000163745821426384 0.000681758302639947 0.000760314875933669"/>
      <geom pos="-0.0106413 -0.0134314 -0.183525" quat="1 0 0 0" type="mesh" rgba="1 0.6 0 1" mesh="handle" contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
      <site name="tcp_site" pos="-0.000241398 0.0187737 -0.174953" quat="0.2705980500730985 -0.6532814824381883 0.6532814824381882 0.2705980500730985" size="0.005" rgba="1 0 0 1"/>
      <body name="trigger" pos="1e-05 0.0542396 -0.0863733" quat="1 0 0 0">
        <inertial pos="-2.88511e-09 0.000787745 -0.0050943" quat="0.456755099233804 0.539791422054777 0.539791422054777 -0.456755099233804" mass="0.00499911" diaginertia="2.23966163220921e-07 6.57726e-07 7.14066836779079e-07"/>
        <joint name="gripper" pos="0 0 0" axis="1 0 0" range="-0.680678 0"/>
        <geom pos="-0.0106913 -0.0680085 -0.0984379" quat="1 0 0 0" type="mesh" rgba="0.764706 0.878431 0.945098 1" mesh="trigger" contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
